=== ITEM 17: twin delivery — exactly ONE effect per logical event ===
=== commit: 1f5458dbeeaff116c10e57cbe2a77ac23a5da0b8 ===

The orchestrator has TWO "ticket done" entry paths that can both fire for one
logical transition: handleTicketDoneUnified (Jira webhook) and handleTicketDone
(DynamoDB stream). Item 17 asks for the count===1 assertion that proves a twin
delivery does not double-act. Three layers assert it, at three different grains.

--- LAYER 1 / shape: the two paths PUBLISH the same detail ---
lambda/orchestrator/verdict-contract.test.mjs:507
  describe("verdict-contract — the agent.complete twins stay identical (TEAM-4246 D1)")

  :519  it("produce byte-identical agent.complete details for the same input")
  :521  expect(enrichCompleteDetail(webhookTwinBase, info))
          .toEqual(enrichCompleteDetail(streamTwinBase, info));

  and the count===1-style structural pin that keeps that meaningful:
  :523  it("both agent.complete publish sites in index.mjs route through enrichCompleteDetail")
  :538  expect(sites.length).toBe(2);          <-- EXACTLY TWO publish sites, no third
  :540-542  for (const at of sites) {
              expect(window).toContain("enrichCompleteDetail(");
              expect(window).toContain("resolveVerdictInfo(");
            }
  This is a source grep over index.mjs, deliberately: cost-report and the twins
  cannot import each other (deploy.sh ships single-file zips), so drift is caught
  by reading the file rather than by importing it. `sites.length).toBe(2)` is the
  assertion that fails if someone adds a third agent.complete publish that skips
  enrichment.

--- LAYER 2 / effect: concurrent twins create exactly ONE ticket ---
lambda/orchestrator/live-reverify.test.mjs:565
  it("two concurrent Dones for the same fix → file exactly ONE re-verify ticket")

  :569-573  two onFixDone calls in Promise.all, sharing the DynamoDB fake but NOT
            the in-memory workflow row — "which is how a stream redelivery and
            its webhook twin actually arrive"
  :575  expect(created(calls)).toHaveLength(1);      <-- THE count===1 ASSERTION
  :576  expect(calls.claims).toHaveLength(2);        <-- both DID race for the slot
  :578  expect(winner).toEqual({ action: "created", reverifyTicketId: REVERIFY,
                                sha7: SHA7, unverified: false });
  :579  expect(loser.action).toBe("already");
  :582  expect(loser.pendingClaim).toBe(true);
  :583  expect(calls.addBlockers).toHaveLength(0);
  Non-vacuity is the :576 line: the test proves BOTH invocations reached the
  claim (2 claims) and only one created (1 ticket). A test that merely asserted
  "1 created" could pass if the second call never ran at all.

  :586  it("a redelivery after the winner persisted returns the winner's ticket, not a new one")
  :604  expect(second).toEqual({ action: "already", reverifyTicketId: REVERIFY,
                                 sha7: SHA7, unverified: false, pendingClaim: false });
  :605  expect(created(calls)).toHaveLength(1);      <-- count===1, COLD container
  The cold-invocation variant matters because the in-memory free check cannot
  help there: only the DynamoDB claim can, which is the path a real redelivery
  minutes later takes.

--- LAYER 3 / cascade: a concurrent last-blocker re-wake fires once ---
lambda/orchestrator/cascade.test.mjs:518
  "TEAM-3684 Finding 2 — idempotent in_review re-wake. Concurrent last-blocker …"
  :523  describe("Finding 2 — idempotent in_review re-wake")
  :546  it("reawakenGate truthy → EXACTLY one review.reawakened + metric counts one")
  :561  expect(eventsOfType(publishEvent, "review.reawakened")).toHaveLength(1);
  :562  expect(records[0].CascadeReviewReawaken).toBe(1);
  Two independent count===1s: the event AND the CloudWatch metric. The metric one
  is the stronger of the pair — an event deduped downstream would still show 1
  event, but a double-executed code path would emit the metric twice.

=== ITEM 17 VERDICT: ASSERTED at all three grains ===
  shape (detail equality + exactly-2-publish-sites), effect (1 ticket from 2
  racing claims, warm AND cold), cascade (1 event + 1 metric datapoint).
  The twin-delivery contract is genuinely pinned, not assumed.

  Honest limit: layer 1 compares enrichCompleteDetail applied to two literal
  objects that the test itself declares (:516-517), copied by hand from the two
  call sites. They are identical BY CONSTRUCTION in the test file, so :521 can
  never fail on its own — its value is entirely in the :538 grep that keeps the
  literals honest. The comment at :513-515 says so outright ("if either site's
  literal drifts, this test's copy goes stale FIRST"). So the real guard against
  twin drift is the source grep, not the equality.

